cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  draughtsman: readable architecture figures for PyTorch
  models, with a coverage check
message: >-
  If you use a figure this produced, cite the software. The
  figure itself is not the citable claim — every number in it
  is read from a trace of your model, and the abstraction is
  a judgement recorded in a spec file that travels with the
  figure. Cite that spec alongside the figure if the grouping
  matters to your argument.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: DeFazio
    given-names: Richard A.
# THE CONCEPT DOI, NOT THE VERSION ONE. Zenodo mints both: a version DOI for
# each release, and a concept DOI that always resolves to the newest. A citation
# that pins a version goes stale the moment there is another; this one does not.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22286341
    description: Concept DOI — always resolves to the latest release.
license: BSD-3-Clause
repository-code: 'https://github.com/syncytium2/draughtsman'
abstract: >-
  A three-stage tool for drawing neural network architecture
  figures. `trace` records facts from torch.jit.trace — every
  shape, parameter count and operation, with nothing inferred.
  `abstract` asks an agent for a layout spec: which operations
  collapse into a stage, what to call it, and where the graph
  forks and rejoins — groupings and human names, never a
  quantity. `render` turns the two into SVG deterministically,
  looking every number up from the trace by node id, so an
  agent cannot put a figure's parameter count in a figure.
  A coverage check then proves no traced operation was
  silently dropped, and further checks refuse an arrow the
  graph does not contain, a parameter counted twice through a
  tied weight, a repetition count that does not tile, and a
  glyph whose axes do not come from one tensor. It has no
  runtime dependencies: layout is implemented here rather than
  delegated, so drawing a figure needs neither torch nor a
  system binary.
keywords:
  - neural network visualisation
  - architecture diagram
  - PyTorch
  - research software
  - reproducible figures
